# Operations and deployment

## Local setup

Use Python 3.12 and `uv`. Run `uv lock` once after generation, commit `uv.lock`,
then use `uv sync --locked --group dev --group docs`. Run `make test lint security
docs-build`, `uv run pre-commit install`, and `make demo`. The local lockfile controls
local Python. Bundle job environments separately install the built wheel and exact
runtime dependencies exported by `scripts/export_runtime.py` from the lockfile.
The serverless pipeline installs that same wheel and dependencies. Spark is supplied
by Databricks, not installed in the local environment. Environment version 4 is a
configurable documented baseline; confirm support in your workspace.

## Workspace setup (requires explicit authorization)

Choose the CLI profile yourself; `make` refuses an empty PROFILE. Generation does
not contact Databricks or create any resources. Copier records a catalog name and
whether each target should create a catalog or use an existing one. Creating a catalog
requires `CREATE CATALOG` privileges. If a managed storage URL is specified, the bundle
creates a managed catalog resource at that location. If the URL is blank, a serverless
bundle job runs `CREATE CATALOG IF NOT EXISTS` during deployment and the deployment
waits for it before creating the four schemas. In a serverless workspace this uses
Databricks Default Storage; a workspace with metastore-managed storage may use its
metastore root. The SQL path reuses a catalog with the chosen name if it already exists.

The SQL-created catalog is created by the deployment job and is not managed as a bundle
catalog resource. Deleting the bundle does not delete that catalog. The job reruns on
deployment. Configure workspace-catalog bindings and grants, especially for PROD.
Databricks Default Storage requires a serverless workspace and serverless compute. See
[default storage](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/storage/default-storage).

For an existing catalog, choose whether the bundle should **create** the four schemas
or **use existing** schemas. Existing schemas are not taken over by the bundle and
must already be named `lz`, `bronze`, `silver`, and `gold`. For a new catalog the
bundle creates those four schemas. Bundle-managed catalog resources and schemas are protected
against bundle destroy; creating resources still requires appropriate privileges. A fresh
catalog may also have Databricks system-created schemas such as `default` and
`information_schema`.

The project owns a managed volume named `{{ package_name }}_files` in `lz`. If it
already exists, bind it to the bundle before deploying, or use a different volume
name consistently. To bind an existing volume, use its full Unity Catalog name:

```bash
databricks bundle deployment bind landing_files my_dev_catalog.lz.{{ package_name }}_files \
  --target dev --profile your-profile
```

The bundle also creates jobs, pipelines and an experiment.
Projects sharing a catalog must coordinate table and model names and permissions.

Copier writes the DEV workspace and catalog into `databricks.yml`:

```yaml
targets:
  dev:
    workspace:
      host: {{ dev_workspace_host.rstrip('/') | to_json }}
    variables:
      catalog: {{ dev_catalog | to_json }}
```

The top-level `variables.catalog` declares the variable; each target selects its own
value. QA/PROD catalogs and separate workspace URLs can be supplied during generation
or configured later. In the Databricks VS Code extension select the **dev** target
and an authentication profile. `BUNDLE_VAR_catalog` or an uncommitted
`.databricks/bundle/<target>/variable-overrides.json` can override the catalog; use
the same value for validation, deployment and execution. No credentials are stored
in Copier answers.

For QA/PROD also set `BUNDLE_VAR_run_as` to the service principal application ID,
`BUNDLE_VAR_deploy_root` to a restricted deployment folder, and
`BUNDLE_VAR_model_version` to the approved numeric destination version. CI's protected
Environment must define `DATABRICKS_HOST` matching the selected target; its catalog override remains supported. No credentials are stored in Copier answers.

```bash
make bundle-validate PROFILE=your-profile TARGET=dev
make bundle-deploy PROFILE=your-profile TARGET=dev
# Creates synthetic input files in the chosen DEV catalog; run once only.
databricks bundle run seed_dev --target dev --profile your-profile
make integration-dev PROFILE=your-profile
```

Integration runs the deployed data pipeline, training, batch inference, monitoring and
remote assertions. This changes UC data and registers a model. Do not point it at QA/PROD.
The deploy builds the wheel and resolves remote dependencies first. No remote resources
are necessary for local tests, linting or documentation. `scripts/check_bundle.py` is an
offline shape/CLI-schema check; it does not replace authenticated `bundle validate`.

## CI and releases

Local CI runs on pull requests without secrets. Secret scanning uses the Gitleaks CLI
without a paid action. DEV integration is a separate manual workflow in a protected
`dev` Environment; it deploys the reviewed default-branch commit and runs integration
against pre-existing DEV input. QA and PROD deployments use a manual release workflow
restricted to the default branch and a protected `qa` or `prod` Environment. Set required
reviewers and deployment branch restrictions on `prod` in GitHub settings: YAML alone
cannot enforce human approval. Until protections are configured, do not enable releases.

Environment variables: `DATABRICKS_HOST`, `DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID`, `CATALOG`,
`RUN_AS`, `DEPLOY_ROOT`, `MODEL_VERSION`; use OAuth workload identity federation with the
GitHub Environment subject and `id-token: write`. No long-lived token is required.
QA deployment runs the data pipeline and then inference/monitoring/smoke against an
already promoted numeric model version and representative QA input. It does not retrain.
QA data refresh defaults to 02:00 UTC daily with an overridable Quartz cron expression.
Inference defaults to 03:00 UTC and monitoring to 04:00 UTC. Schedules are independent;
for larger workloads replace these timing assumptions with task dependencies. PROD schedules stay paused until deliberately enabled.
Set job failure recipients through the bundle notification variable.

## Verified syntax and remaining validation

`template-basis.yml` is the template's provenance snapshot: review date, Databricks
skill pack version, each reviewed `SKILL.md` version/hash, tested CLI and validation
scope. A null skill version means the entrypoint does not declare a version; its
SHA-256 still identifies the reviewed bytes. Hashes cover entrypoints, not their
referenced files or the entire extension package.

The snapshot updates with `copier update`. It describes upstream template verification,
not verification of this project's changes or workspace. Keep project-specific CLI
upgrades, validation dates and workspace results in this operations guide. The source
template version is recorded separately in `.copier-answers.yml` for Git-based sources.
An installed extension update does not modify the snapshot or authorize restructuring.

The resource layout follows the current `default-python` starter conventions; local
scaffolding did not invoke an authenticated starter or use a workspace profile.
Local test results do not imply that DEV deployment, serverless environments,
workspace permissions or data profiling have been exercised. Complete DEV integration
before tagging the first remotely verified template release.

References: [bundle templates](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/templates),
[serverless bundle examples](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/examples),
[Unity Catalog models](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/manage-model-lifecycle/),
[Python pipelines](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ldp/developer/python-dev),
[serving monitoring](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/monitor-diagnose-endpoints).

Connection configuration was reviewed on 28 October 2026 against the official
[bundle variable precedence](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/bundles/variables)
and [VS Code configuration](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/vscode-ext/configure)
guidance. Generated DEV defaults are checked without using workspace credentials.

## Updating from an earlier bundle layout

If an older generated repository defined `seed_dev`, `integration_dev` or the
`training` experiment inside `databricks.yml`, these definitions now live under
`resources/`. When updating with Copier, review any changes or conflicts in
`databricks.yml` and transfer project-specific edits to the corresponding resource
file. The experiment resource key changed from `training` to `training_experiment` to
avoid a duplicate key with the training job; review its existing state binding.
Keep a single definition for each resource. Shared jobs remain in
`resources/workflows.jobs.yml`.

## Migrating from the prefixed schema layout

**Do not deploy an updated project until its existing bundle state and data have been
reviewed.** Removing or changing formerly managed schema and volume definitions can
make a deployment plan propose destructive operations. `prevent_destroy` on the new
resources does not protect resources removed from the new configuration. Back up
the data and state, inspect a bundle plan and arrange any necessary state migration
with the platform team before deployment.

This template version changes paths and does **not** migrate existing data. The old
`<namespace>_landing`, `_bronze`, `_silver`, `_gold`, `_models`, `_predictions`,
and `_monitoring` schemas and their existing tables, volume and registered models
remain where they are. The new workloads read and write `lz`, `bronze`, `silver`,
and `gold`, with project-prefixed object names. Plan data and model migration,
review deployment state and resource bindings, and validate in DEV before deploying.
Do not destroy old resources or assume model versions exist at the new names.
For existing four-schema catalogs select **use existing** to avoid attempting to
create or assume ownership of shared schemas. Review Copier conflicts and the
removed `namespace` overrides and CI variables.
